  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B001",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks, enabling unprecedented precision in timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B021",
    "B037"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are a mature fundamental advance: carefully controlled atoms in a magic-wavelength lattice convert an exquisite quantum transition into a reproducible measurement standard. The achievement joins a clear physical concept to decisive experimental realization, has been independently extended by many laboratories, and creates genuinely new access to relativistic and other fundamental tests. Katori and Ye remain the compact and convincing central attribution.",
    "case_against": "The citation must recognize the lattice-clock breakthrough itself rather than promise every later application. The field also includes important contributors in other clock architectures and many laboratories, so the historical boundary of the core invention needs documentation.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes that Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's experimental development provide an unusually clear allocation of credit, with consequences that are already unmistakable.",
        "response": "I agree. That compact pairing is a substantial advantage: it connects a specific insight in atom-light control to a body of experimental work whose precision and reproducibility are no longer dependent on one laboratory."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce asks whether optical lattice clocks should be regarded chiefly as an exceptional technical development rather than a fundamental discovery.",
        "response": "The distinction is useful, but I would not draw it too sharply here. The decisive advance is not merely improved engineering; it made the quantum phase evolution of trapped atoms a qualitatively more controllable and universal probe. Its value for tests of fundamental physics is already demonstrated, while a prize need not await every prospective metrological application."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll notes that the eventual replacement or redefinition of current time standards remains unsettled.",
        "response": "That is a real uncertainty, but it is not central to the scientific case. A redefinition would be a useful institutional consequence, whereas the underlying clock physics and its independent validation are already fully mature."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How narrowly the historical record can separate the original magic-wavelength lattice-clock achievement from related clock architectures and later applications.",
      "Whether a final citation should mention time standards explicitly before a formal redefinition of the second."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
